    "latex_code": "\\documentclass[twocolumn]{nature}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amsfonts,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{cite}\n\n% Journal metadata specification\n% Publisher: Nature Portfolio | Category: Biology & Genetics\n% ISSN: 1087-0156 | DOI: 10.1038/s41587-024-02194-x\n\n\\title{High-throughput single-cell epigenomic profiling via combinatorial cellular barcoding}\n\\author{Dr. Sarah E. Collins, Dr. Michael R. Chang, and Prof. Jennifer A. Doudna\\\\\n\\small Innovative Genomics Institute, UC Berkeley, Berkeley, CA; Department of Bioengineering, Stanford University, Stanford, CA}\n\\date{Vol. 42, No. 5, pp. 612\u2013624, May 2024}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nCharacterizing regulatory chromatin landscapes at single-cell resolution requires high cellular throughput without cross-contamination. Here we describe split-pool transposome tagmentation (sci-ATAC-v3), enabling profiling of over 1.2 million single nuclei in a single two-day workflow. We benchmark the platform across heterogeneous mammalian tissues, resolving rare cell types and cell-type specific transcription factor binding footprints with high fidelity.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\textbf{Keywords:} Single-Cell ATAC-seq, Chromatin Accessibility, High-Throughput Genomics, Split-Pool Barcoding, Regulatory Elements\n\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nDeciphering mammalian gene regulation requires dissecting open chromatin states across millions of individual cells composing complex organ systems.\n\n\\section{Microfluidics-Free Split-Pool Barcoding}\nNuclei are partitioned across 384-well plates for indexed Tn5 transposase insertion, pooled, and redistributed through successive split-pool ligations to append unique molecular indices without specialized droplet microfluidics.\n\n\\section{Tagmentation Kinetics Formulation}\nThe expected barcode collision rate $C(N, K)$ for $N$ sequenced cells distributed randomly across $K$ combinatorially indexed partitions follows Poisson occupancy statistics:\n\\begin{equation}\n    C(N, K) = 1 - \\exp\\left( -\\frac{N(N-1)}{2K} \\right) + \\mathcal{O}\\left( \\frac{N^3}{K^2} \\right)\n\\end{equation}\nwhich remains below $0.4\\%$ for $K = 384^3$ barcode combinations at $N = 10^6$.\n\n\\section{Results & Tissue Atlas}\nWe mapped 42 distinct hematopoietic lineages from human bone marrow biopsies, demonstrating identification of rare progenitor populations (<0.05% abundance).\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nSplit-pool barcoding eliminates equipment barriers, facilitating population-scale single-cell epigenomic studies.\n\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Empirical Validation and Benchmark Results across Experimental Trials}\n\\label{tab:results}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Configuration / Pipeline} & \\textbf{Precision (\\%)} & \\textbf{Recall (\\%)} & \\textbf{F1 Score} & \\textbf{Throughput} \\\\\n\\midrule\nStandard Baseline Model            & 87.4 $\\pm$ 0.3          & 85.1 $\\pm$ 0.4       & 86.2              & 1,420 ops/s \\\\\nIterative Refinement Approach      & 91.2 $\\pm$ 0.2          & 89.8 $\\pm$ 0.3       & 90.5              & 2,180 ops/s \\\\\n\\textbf{Our Authentic Framework}   & \\textbf{96.8 $\\pm$ 0.1} & \\textbf{95.4 $\\pm$ 0.2}& \\textbf{96.1}     & \\textbf{4,850 ops/s} \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\n\\section*{Acknowledgments}\nThe authors acknowledge high-performance computing resources, institutional laboratory access, and funding provided by national research grants in support of this work.\n\n\\bibliographystyle{plain}\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\end{document}\n",
