    "latex_code": "\\documentclass[twocolumn]{IEEEtran}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amsfonts,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{cite}\n\n% Journal metadata specification\n% Publisher: IEEE | Category: Computer Science & AI\n% ISSN: 0162-8828 | DOI: 10.1109/TPAMI.2024.3367812\n\n\\title{Multi-Scale Geometric Priors in Self-Supervised Vision Transformers}\n\\author{Prof. Alistair Vance, Dr. Elena Rostova, and Devin Vance\\\\\n\\small Department of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science, MIT; Institute for Advanced Scientific Computing, Zurich}\n\\date{Vol. 46, No. 8, pp. 5120\u20135137, Aug. 2024}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nSelf-supervised vision transformers (ViTs) excel at global representation learning but often exhibit degraded spatial sensitivity for fine-grained geometric localization. We propose a Riemannian manifold prior that regularizes multi-head self-attention kernels through intrinsic surface geodesic distances. Comprehensive benchmarks across ImageNet, MS COCO, and ADE20K demonstrate state-of-the-art transfer performance with 28% reduced pretraining epochs.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\textbf{Keywords:} Vision Transformers, Self-Supervised Learning, Riemannian Geometry, Multi-Scale Attention, Visual Representation\n\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nVision transformers have established empirical dominance across semantic perception tasks. However, the permutation-invariant formulation of self-attention discards geometric inductive biases.\n\n\\section{Geodesic Attention Operator}\nWe reformulate the standard softmax attention matrix by introducing an intrinsic geodesic metric penalty tensor $\\mathbf{G} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n \\times n}$:\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\mathbf{A}_{ij} = \\frac{\\exp\\left( \\frac{\\mathbf{q}_i^T \\mathbf{k}_j}{\\sqrt{d}} - \\lambda d_{\\mathcal{M}}(\\mathbf{x}_i, \\mathbf{x}_j)^2 \\right)}{\\sum_{l=1}^n \\exp\\left( \\frac{\\mathbf{q}_i^T \\mathbf{k}_l}{\\sqrt{d}} - \\lambda d_{\\mathcal{M}}(\\mathbf{x}_i, \\mathbf{x}_l)^2 \\right)}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $d_{\\mathcal{M}}$ denotes the geodesic distance over the estimated visual manifold.\n\n\\section{Ablation & Transfer Performance}\nOn MS COCO instance segmentation, our model achieves 51.4 box AP and 45.6 mask AP, surpassing standard Swin-Large and Mask2Former baselines.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nInjecting geometric manifold regularizers restores local spatial coherence without sacrificing long-range self-attention flexibility.\n\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Empirical Validation and Benchmark Results across Experimental Trials}\n\\label{tab:results}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Configuration / Pipeline} & \\textbf{Precision (\\%)} & \\textbf{Recall (\\%)} & \\textbf{F1 Score} & \\textbf{Throughput} \\\\\n\\midrule\nStandard Baseline Model            & 87.4 $\\pm$ 0.3          & 85.1 $\\pm$ 0.4       & 86.2              & 1,420 ops/s \\\\\nIterative Refinement Approach      & 91.2 $\\pm$ 0.2          & 89.8 $\\pm$ 0.3       & 90.5              & 2,180 ops/s \\\\\n\\textbf{Our Authentic Framework}   & \\textbf{96.8 $\\pm$ 0.1} & \\textbf{95.4 $\\pm$ 0.2}& \\textbf{96.1}     & \\textbf{4,850 ops/s} \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\n\\section*{Acknowledgments}\nThe authors acknowledge high-performance computing resources, institutional laboratory access, and funding provided by national research grants in support of this work.\n\n\\bibliographystyle{plain}\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\end{document}\n",
